encyclopedia :trellin do
  name "Trellin"
  summary "Trellin are a sapient people whose cartilaginous throat fans receive and produce signal-band emission alongside ordinary speech. With training, tonal memory can preserve a transmission and reproduce its voice as layered chords."
  kind :lifeform
  origin_blurb "Cartilaginous throat fans receive and produce signal-band emission; trained tonal memory can reproduce a received voice in layered chords."
  subkind :sapient_species
  status :complete
  topics :species, :archives, :"signal-freq"
  prevalence :uncommon
  available_globally
  registry true
  origin "A people of the signal country, documented through the ring era in the relay guilds' service — the recovered guild rolls carry trellin names from the network's first pages"
  biology "Cartilaginous throat fans that receive and produce signal-band emission alongside ordinary speech; trained trellin hold received transmissions in tonal memory and reproduce them voice-true as layered chords"
  lifespan "About 70–120 years"
  resonance_relation "Native to the signal band — trellin hear traffic the way sighted peoples see light, and their fans both receive and transmit at short range, which the relay trades use where lines fail"

  descriptive_identity(
    appearance: "Human-scaled, slender-necked, with the folded throat fans " \
                "visible as ribbed pleating from jaw to collarbone — spread " \
                "in reception, rippling in transmission, and held formally " \
                "flat for silence, which among trellin is a posture rather " \
                "than an absence.",
    senses: "The fans distinguish signal traffic, direction, and local " \
            "dialects at short range. Networks with heavy overlap can be " \
            "painfully loud until the fans fold or the traffic is damped.",
    behavior: "Fans spread during reception, ripple during transmission, and " \
              "fold flat to reduce exposure. Relay and keeper schools teach " \
              "exact delivery disciplines; other trellin may never train " \
              "tonal memory beyond ordinary conversation."
  )

  cue "The court asks for the testimony and the trellin keeper spreads her fans and delivers it — the dead witness's own voice in layered chord, cadence and hesitation intact — and the registrar marks the record heard rather than read."
  cue "On the dead-band route the courier's fans ripple at the waystation and the relay's backlog crosses the broken stretch through her — received, held, and re-sent — while the linesmen work on the repair."
  affordance "A trained trellin can carry a transmission through a dead band, reproduce recorded testimony voice-true, and distinguish overlapping local signal traffic."
  variation "Relay and keeper schools develop tonal memory for routes, archives, and courts. Singing schools use related chord forms for music and history; most trellin learn neither as an occupation."
  variation "Fan dialects differ by region as accents do, and trellin place each other's training lineages at a phrase — the archive schools' formal fans, the route trades' clipped working forms, and the old ring-pattern chords kept ceremonially by both."

  prose <<~PROSE
    Trellin have ribbed cartilaginous fans running from jaw to collarbone. Spread fans receive signal-band emission and distinguish traffic, direction, and local dialect; rippling tissue produces short-range transmission beside ordinary speech. Folding the fans flat reduces reception in a crowded band. Heavy overlapping traffic can otherwise be painful and makes damped rooms an ordinary accommodation in relay districts.

    Tonal memory is trainable. A practiced trellin can receive a transmission, hold its cadence, hesitation, and timbre, and reproduce it as layered chords. Relay guilds employed trellin keepers throughout the ring era and maintained drills for line failure. During the Glassfall and Famine, trained couriers carried #{encyclopedia_ref :white_signal_cloth, "Fold"} across dead bands; surviving route schools preserve records and chord forms from those walks.

    Present-day keeper schools teach exact delivery, witness handling, and the limits of voice-true recall. Courts distinguish a record read from a record heard and require witnesses when a keeper fixes testimony into tonal memory. Relay schools train shorter retention and repeated handoff along broken lines. Related chord forms also carry music and history in #{encyclopedia_ref :music_in_kaleidos, "the Answering Phrase"}. These institutions are prominent trellin traditions, but the sensory anatomy does not assign a person to them.
  PROSE
end
